{"slug": "ttl-vs-cmos-logic-families", "targets": ["compare ttl and cmos"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Compare TTL and CMOS: Parameter Quick Reference</h2><p><strong>Compare TTL and CMOS</strong> on any datasheet and the gap comes from the transistors. TTL uses bipolar transistors that draw base current all the time, while CMOS pairs P channel and N channel MOSFETs so one is always off and static current is almost zero.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Parameter</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">TTL (74 series)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">CMOS (4000 and 74HC)</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Transistor type</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Bipolar junction</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Complementary MOSFET</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Supply voltage</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">5 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3 to 15 V (4000), 2 to 6 V (74HC)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">VIH minimum at 5 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2.0 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">About 3.5 V (74HC)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">VIL maximum at 5 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.8 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">About 1.5 V (74HC)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Noise margin at 5 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.4 V</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">About 1.4 V (74HC)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Static power per gate</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">About 10 mW</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">In the nanowatt range</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Dynamic power</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Small share of total</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">P = C x V<sup>2</sup> x f, rises with frequency</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Input current</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Significant, limits fan out</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Almost zero, high fan out</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Unused inputs</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Float high but should be tied</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Must be tied to VCC or ground</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">ESD sensitivity</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Low</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">High, handle with care</td></tr></table></div><p>When you compare TTL and CMOS for a new design, CMOS normally wins on power and noise margin. TTL compatible CMOS such as 74HCT accepts TTL input levels, which makes it the usual choice when mixing the two families.</p>"}